      "inspected_2026_10_16": true,
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "locator": "Noul A Noul",
          "text": "Noul A Noul answer is a single probability p p p that the answer is yes, and TypeSafe returns no separate confidence for it. The probability already carries the uncertainty: a value near 0.5 is the model saying it is unsure, and the Noul page covers how to threshold on it directly. If you want a confidence-style number anyway, for example to gate Nouls and Choices with the same code, use the distance from 0.5: confidence = ∣ 2 p − 1 ∣ \\text{confidence} = |2p - 1| confidence = ∣2 p − 1∣ This gives 0 at p = 0.5 p = 0.5 p = 0.5 and 1 at p = 0 p = "
        },
        {
          "locator": "Choice A Choice",
          "text": "Choice A Choice extends the same idea to any number of options. For a Choice with n n n options, where p max ⁡ p_{\\max} p m a x ​ is the probability of the selected option: confidence = p max ⁡ − 1 n 1 − 1 n \\text{confidence} = \\frac{p_{\\max} - \\frac{1}{n}}{1 - \\frac{1}{n}} confidence = 1 − n 1 ​ p m a x ​ − n 1 ​ ​ This measures how far the top probability sits above an even split of 1 n \\frac{1}{n} n 1 ​ per option, on a scale where the even split is 0 and certainty is 1. Only the top probability counts, so ( 0.6 , 0.3 , 0.1 ) (0.6, 0.3, 0.1)"
        }
